BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261106T170000Z
DTEND:20261106T180000Z
LOCATION:355 North Eagleson Avenue
SUMMARY:IKS Colloquium - Heangjin Park
DESCRIPTION:IKS Fall 2026 Colloquium SeriesHeangjin Park  Department of A
 sian Languages and Cultures  University of Wisconsin-Madison  "Korean" Ki
 mchi: A National\, Global\, and Diasporic CommodityFriday\, November 612:
 00-1:00pmGISB 2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave  How has kimchi\, a fermented veg
 etable dish beloved by Koreans and beyond\, become a national dish that s
 ymbolizes Korean culture? How has its Koreanness transformed through glob
 al production and circulation? And what does it mean for the Korean diasp
 ora\, who navigate political identity and economic opportunity from perip
 heral positions? This talk demonstrates how mundane commodities mediate t
 he complex interplay among global capitalism\, nationalism\, and diaspori
 c mobility across South Korea and China. Drawing on archival and ethnogra
 phic research into kimchi's commercial production and circulation\, espec
 ially the Korean Chinese (chosonjok/chaoxianzu) kimchi business\, this ta
 lk illuminates the political economic dynamics that reshape the multiface
 ted implications of "Korea."  Open to the public. A light lunch will be s
 erved.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;">  IKS Fall 
 2026 Colloquium Series</h5><h6 style="text-align: center\;"><br /><span c
 lass="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off font-feature-cal
 t-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none">Heangjin Park</span><
 /h6><p style="text-align: center\;">  Department of Asian Languages and C
 ultures<br />  University of Wisconsin-Madison</p><p style="text-align: c
 enter\;">  "Korean" Kimchi: A National\, Global\, and Diasporic Commodity
 </p><p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Friday\, November 6</strong><
 /p><p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>12:00-1:00pm</strong></p><p st
 yle="text-align: center\;"><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font
 -feature-clig-off font-feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-striket
 hrough-none"><strong>GISB 2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave</strong><br /><br /> 
  How has kimchi\, a fermented vegetable dish beloved by Koreans and beyon
 d\, become a national dish that symbolizes Korean culture? How has its Ko
 reanness transformed through global production and circulation? And what 
 does it mean for the Korean diaspora\, who navigate political identity an
 d economic opportunity from peripheral positions? This talk demonstrates 
 how mundane commodities mediate the complex interplay among global capita
 lism\, nationalism\, and diasporic mobility across South Korea and China.
  Drawing on archival and ethnographic research into kimchi's commercial p
 roduction and circulation\, especially the Korean Chinese (chosonjok/chao
 xianzu) kimchi business\, this talk illuminates the political economic dy
 namics that reshape the multifaceted implications of "Korea."<br /><br />
   Open to the public. A light lunch will be served.</span></p>
