BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261110T170000Z
DTEND:20261110T180000Z
LOCATION:Teams Webinar &amp\; The Regenstrief Institute
GEO:39.779628;-86.180457
SUMMARY:Global Health Research Speaker Series - Dr. Alka Khaitan
DESCRIPTION:Join the IU Center for Global Health and the Regenstrief Inst
 itute's Center for Health Services Research for this quarter's Global Hea
 lth Research Speaker Series session featuring&#160\;Alka Khaitan\, MD (ht
 tps://medicine.iu.edu/faculty/43672/khaitan-alka)  Professor of Clinical 
 Pediatrics\, Indiana University School of Medicine and Ryan White Center 
 for Pediatric Infectious Diseases and Global Health"Beyond Viral Suppress
 ion: Immune Dysregulation and HIV Persistence in Perinatal HIV"  Antiretr
 oviral therapy has transformed perinatal HIV into a chronic\, treatable c
 ondition\, yet viral suppression does not fully restore immune health or 
 eliminate persistent HIV reservoirs. This talk will explore the long-term
  immunologic consequences of perinatal HIV and how immune dysregulation m
 ay intersect with HIV persistence. Research from a Kenyan cohort of child
 ren with perinatal HIV will provide insight into how the immunologic effe
 cts of HIV acquired early in life may persist despite treatment and relat
 e to long-term health outcomes and barriers to HIV cure.  Dr. Khaitan is 
 a professor of clinical pediatrics in the Division of Pediatric Infectiou
 s Diseases at Indiana University School of Medicine and the Ryan White Ce
 nter for Pediatric Infectious Diseases and Global Health. Her research fo
 cuses on the long-term immunologic consequences of perinatal HIV\, includ
 ing chronic inflammation\, immune activation and exhaustion and viral per
 sistence. She leads NIH-funded research in the United States and Kenya ex
 amining how the timing and effectiveness of antiretroviral therapy shape 
 immune health and long-term outcomes in children with HIV. She is also in
 volved in pediatric HIV cure research through the IMPAACT Network and ser
 ves on the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Pediatric Antiret
 roviral Guidelines Panel.  This session is being held both virtually (via
  Teams webinar) and in-person. If you plan to attend the session in-perso
 n at the Regenstrief Institute you must register by Thursday\, November 5
 th at 10 am Eastern Time and indicate this choice when you register. &#16
 0\;  Additionally\, please take note of the following in-person details:I
 n-Person Location: Regenstrief Institute (Room 209/210)\, 1101 W. 10th St
 reet\, Indianapolis (corner of 10th Street and Wilson Street)  Parking: P
 ublic (for fee) parking is available in the Wilson Street Garage which is
  adjacent to the building.  Building Security: Regenstrief maintains a se
 cure building entrance. Event support from the Center for Global Health a
 nd the Center for Health Services Research will be onsite to assist with 
 entry and other event logistics.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Join the IU Center for Global Health an
 d the Regenstrief Institute's Center for Health Services Research for thi
 s quarter's Global Health Research Speaker Series session featuring&#160\
 ;</p><p><a href="https://medicine.iu.edu/faculty/43672/khaitan-alka">Alka
  Khaitan\, MD</a></p><p>  Professor of Clinical Pediatrics\, Indiana Univ
 ersity School of Medicine and Ryan White Center for Pediatric Infectious 
 Diseases and Global Health</p><p><strong>"Beyond Viral Suppression: Immun
 e Dysregulation and HIV Persistence in Perinatal HIV"</strong></p><p>  An
 tiretroviral therapy has transformed perinatal HIV into a chronic\, treat
 able condition\, yet viral suppression does not fully restore immune heal
 th or eliminate persistent HIV reservoirs. This talk will explore the lon
 g-term immunologic consequences of perinatal HIV and how immune dysregula
 tion may intersect with HIV persistence. Research from a Kenyan cohort of
  children with perinatal HIV will provide insight into how the immunologi
 c effects of HIV acquired early in life may persist despite treatment and
  relate to long-term health outcomes and barriers to HIV cure.</p><p>  Dr
 . Khaitan is a professor of clinical pediatrics in the Division of Pediat
 ric Infectious Diseases at Indiana University School of Medicine and the 
 Ryan White Center for Pediatric Infectious Diseases and Global Health. He
 r research focuses on the long-term immunologic consequences of perinatal
  HIV\, including chronic inflammation\, immune activation and exhaustion 
 and viral persistence. She leads NIH-funded research in the United States
  and Kenya examining how the timing and effectiveness of antiretroviral t
 herapy shape immune health and long-term outcomes in children with HIV. S
 he is also involved in pediatric HIV cure research through the IMPAACT Ne
 twork and serves on the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Pedi
 atric Antiretroviral Guidelines Panel.</p><p>  This session is being held
  both virtually (via Teams webinar) and in-person. If you plan to attend 
 the session in-person at the Regenstrief Institute you must register by T
 hursday\, November 5th at 10 am Eastern Time and indicate this choice whe
 n you register. &#160\;</p><p>  Additionally\, please take note of the fo
 llowing in-person details:</p><ul><li>In-Person Location: Regenstrief Ins
 titute (Room 209/210)\, 1101 W. 10th Street\, Indianapolis (corner of 10t
 h Street and Wilson Street)  </li><li>Parking: Public (for fee) parking i
 s available in the Wilson Street Garage which is adjacent to the building
 .  </li><li>Building Security: Regenstrief maintains a secure building en
 trance. Event support from the Center for Global Health and the Center fo
 r Health Services Research will be onsite to assist with entry and other 
 event logistics.  </li></ul>
