LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:Tobias Center Research Seminar: Organizing Developmental Statecraft
DESCRIPTION:This installment of the Tobias Center research seminar series
  features Hong Zhang\, Assistant Professor in the IU Department of Intern
 ational Studies. Her talk will explore her upcoming book project\, Organi
 zing Developmental Statecraft: Nodal Bureaucratic Coordination in China's
  Global Economic Strategy.Abstract:&#160\;China is both a rising power in
 ternationally and a developing economy confronting persistent domestic de
 velopment challenges\, in particular regional inequality and fragmentatio
 n. This book project argues that China's efforts to build structural powe
 r in the global economy cannot be properly understood apart from its cont
 inuing project of domestic developmental transformation. I develop the co
 ncept of developmental statecraft to describe state efforts to couple the
 se two processes: advancing domestic developmental objectives while shapi
 ng the external economic networks through which development takes place. 
 Developmental statecraft thus links the integration of markets\, infrastr
 ucture\, production\, and finance across domestic and international scale
 s.  How is developmental statecraft organized? This talk focuses on the n
 odal bureaucratic coordination through China's National Development and R
 eform Commission (NDRC). I argue that the NDRC acts as a nodal agency con
 necting policy domains and actors that are otherwise institutionally frag
 mented. Through programmatic planning\, interministerial coordination\, c
 entral-local linkages\, project gate-keeping\, and international agenda a
 lignment\, these institutional processes help produce strategic coherence
  between domestic developmental agendas and external economic strategy. T
 he analysis identifies bureaucratic coordination capacity as a critical d
 imension of developmental statecraft and shows how the organization of de
 velopment can itself become a source of structural power in the global po
 litical economy.  Lunch will be provided.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This installment of the Tobias Center r
 esearch seminar series features Hong Zhang\, Assistant Professor in the I
 U Department of International Studies. Her talk will explore her upcoming
  book project\, <em>Organizing Developmental Statecraft: Nodal Bureaucrat
 ic Coordination in China's Global Economic Strategy.</em></p><p><strong>A
 bstract:</strong>&#160\;China is both a rising power internationally and 
 a developing economy confronting persistent domestic development challeng
 es\, in particular regional inequality and fragmentation. This book proje
 ct argues that China's efforts to build structural power in the global ec
 onomy cannot be properly understood apart from its continuing project of 
 domestic developmental transformation. I develop the concept of developme
 ntal statecraft to describe state efforts to couple these two processes: 
 advancing domestic developmental objectives while shaping the external ec
 onomic networks through which development takes place. Developmental stat
 ecraft thus links the integration of markets\, infrastructure\, productio
 n\, and finance across domestic and international scales.</p><p>  How is 
 developmental statecraft organized? This talk focuses on the nodal bureau
 cratic coordination through China's National Development and Reform Commi
 ssion (NDRC). I argue that the NDRC acts as a nodal agency connecting pol
 icy domains and actors that are otherwise institutionally fragmented. Thr
 ough programmatic planning\, interministerial coordination\, central-loca
 l linkages\, project gate-keeping\, and international agenda alignment\, 
 these institutional processes help produce strategic coherence between do
 mestic developmental agendas and external economic strategy. The analysis
  identifies bureaucratic coordination capacity as a critical dimension of
  developmental statecraft and shows how the organization of development c
 an itself become a source of structural power in the global political eco
 nomy.</p><p>  Lunch will be provided.</p>
